WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.790 --> 00:00:04.749 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  Razi Jalil loved life being surrounded  by her adoring family meant everything  to her she was a very strong loving very  caring woman she always taught us me and  my siblings always to think of others  she all would have had other people's  interest at heart dying of Kovan 19  gasping Ferrer isolated and alone would  have destroyed rasa as much as the virus  that killed her shumsa walked her mother  into a waiting ambulance when she fell  ill it would be the last time she saw  her mother alive it's not being able to  go and see her even if you just go and  sit with her it's it's a big big thing  just to be able to sit and comfort her  you couldn't do that can anything  prepare you for the loss of a loved one  it's a big hole in your life this  disease is doubly cruel it robs you of  life and your loved ones of a chance to  hold you and say goodbye  you've never been given the opportunity  to say goodbye to your mother that must  have been really difficult and like my  daughter was there she is upset I can't  can't hug her can't comfort her that was  heartbreaking  this family's agony is being repeated in  homes right across the country because  Razia is just one of the 10,000 people  who have lost their lives to covert 19  the first recorded virus death was here  at the Royal Berkshire Hospital in a  little over five short weeks we have  passed a grim milestone a woman in her  70s died on the 5th of March a day after  being admitted within a week nine others  wouldn't make it out of hospital a month  later the death toll stood at almost  5,000 and now less than 40 days are for  the first coronavirus death on the  British soil  the figure has reached over 10,000  people that significance was not lost on  the health secretary we joined the list  of countries who have seen more than  10,000 deaths related to coronavirus  while so many families still mourn their  loved ones now may not be the time to  ask difficult questions about government  strategy except one what bereaved  families will want to know is if more  lives could have been saved  I think I can look at whether the  decisions about at what point we would  into lockdown  at what point we moved away from doing  the case detection and quarantine of  contacts was that done at exactly the  right time and I say these are always  questions that are easy to answer in  hindsight but I think certainly some  people in the public out community would  say a more aggressive approach to the  contact tracing and testing quarantine  of contacts at the beginning and keeping  out up for longer might have made a  difference but I have to say in the 2009  h1n1 flu pandemic people criticized the  government for the reverse for hanging  on too long to the strategy it's not  easy and that difference might have  prevented heartache for families like  Rogers it's really hard for any one of  us to truly comprehend the sheer scale  of this pandemic the number of lives  lost here in the UK try and imagine this  stadium on  matchday packed with fans full of energy  noise and life now think of 10,000 empty  seats each one of these representing a  life lost a life taken or a life cut  short football grounds will fill up  again and slowly very slowly after this  pandemic is finally over our lives will  begin to return to normal but for at  least 10,000 families their lives will  never be the same again  Ashish Joshi sky news

